  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Ramaria rubella, commonly known as the pink coral fungus, is a branched fruiting body that displays delicate coral-like structures in shades of pink and white. This species belongs to the Gomphaceae family and is edible, though it requires proper identification to distinguish from potentially toxic look-alikes.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Coral-like branching structure",
      "description": "The fruiting body has repeatedly branching, upright arms that resemble delicate coral formations, typically reaching 2-4 inches tall.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pink to reddish coloration",
      "description": "The tips and branches display distinctive pink or reddish hues that may fade to pale or whitish toward the base.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Blunt branch tips",
      "description": "The terminal branches end in blunt, rounded tips rather than sharp points, distinguishing it from similar species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ramaria rubella typically fruiting in hardwood and mixed forests throughout New England during summer and early fall, preferring well-established woodland areas with rich soil and adequate moisture.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate, coral-like appearance, Ramaria rubella is edible when properly cooked, though it is rarely commercially harvested and considered a choice edible by experienced foragers in the northeastern United States.",
